<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Free City of Riga</h1><p class="st-meta">1282 CE – 1581 CE (approx.) · capital Riga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest Hanseatic city of Livonia, which fought the Livonian Order and its archbishops for its liberties until it submitted to Poland in 1581.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Riga was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-of-riga">Bishop Albert</a> in 1201 and soon received its own town law. By the late 13th century its merchant council was strong enough to act independently, and it joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> in 1282, controlling trade along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The city was governed by a council of merchants, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> for merchants and craftsmen. Its rivalry with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-order">Livonian Order</a> led to open war from 1297; the city even allied with pagan <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a>, but the Order captured it in 1330. Riga exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>, wax, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> in 1522, one of the first cities outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to do so.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/terra-mariana">Terra Mariana</a> collapsed in 1561, Riga refused to submit and remained a free city for twenty years. In 1581 it accepted the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-bathory">Stephen Báthory</a> of Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and it later became the capital of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/swedish-livonia">Swedish Livonia</a> and then of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/governorate-of-livonia">Governorate of Livonia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-city-of-riga.jpg" alt="Free City of Riga" width="320" height="251"><figcaption>Image: Dirk Jansz van Santen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atlas_Van_der_Hagen-KW1049B10_029-LIVONIA_Vulgo_Lyetland.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1282 CE – 1581 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Riga</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, Lutheranism from 1522</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Low German, Latvian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Riga, Hanseatic city of Riga</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1432&amp;focus=free-city-of-riga" aria-label="Free City of Riga on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5200 245 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free City of Riga lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5520 501L5237 584M5520 501L5539 500M5520 501L5229 694M5520 501L5457 693"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5237 584h0M5539 500h0M5229 694h0M5457 693h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5520 501h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5520 501h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Riga. <a href="/atlas?year=1432&amp;focus=free-city-of-riga">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
